### Step 5 – Constructions for the admissible values

> **\(k=0\).** “The \(n\) vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n\) … cover every point of \(S_n\).” – Correct.

> **\(k=1\).** “Use the \(n-1\) vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n-1\). The only uncovered point is \((n,1)\). The sunny line \(\ell:\; y=2x-2n+1\) passes through \((n,1)\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.” – The line indeed passes through \((n,1)\) and has slope \(2\neq0,\infty,-1\). All other points are covered by the vertical lines. Hence the construction works.

> **\(k=3\).** “Use the \(n-3\) vertical lines \(x=1,\dots ,x=n-3\). The uncovered set is \(T_3\). The three sunny lines  
> \(L_{1}: y=x,\; L_{2}: y=-2x+5,\; L_{3}: y=-\tfrac12x+\tfrac52\)  
> contain respectively \((1,1),(2,2);\;(1,3),(2,1);\;(1,2),(3,1)\), i.e. all six points of \(T_3\).” – Direct substitution confirms each listed point satisfies the corresponding line equation, and the six points are exactly the elements of \(T_3\). The lines are distinct and sunny. Hence the construction is valid.

**Conclusion:** All three constructions are correct and fully verify the required coverage.

---

### Step 6 – Conclusion

> “The only non‑negative integers \(k\) … are \(k\in\{0,1,3\}\).”

Given the correctness of the counting argument and the explicit constructions, the final answer is indeed the complete set of admissible \(k\).

**Conclusion:** The final answer is correct.

