ach listed point satisfies the corresponding line equation, and the six points are exactly the elements of \\(T_3\\). The lines are distinct and sunny. Hence the construction is valid.\n\n**Conclusion:** All three constructions are correct and fully verify the required coverage.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion\n\n> \u201cThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) \u2026 are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d\n\nGiven the correctness of the counting argument and the explicit constructions, the final answer is indeed the complete set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n**Conclusion:** The final answer is correct.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution reaches the correct answer and the main logical structure (reduction, bound, counting, and constructions) is sound. However, the proof contains several **Justification Gaps**: an incomplete treatment of the case \\(p+q=-1,\\;q=1\\) in the bound, a mis\u2011statement about the condition \\(|p+q|=2\\), and an inaccurate claim concerning the bound for \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\). These gaps do not invalidate the final result but prevent the solution from being fully rigorous. Consequently, the solution is **correct but not completely rigorous**."
